<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ateas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 429–339 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ateas was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> in the 4th century BCE who built a powerful realm north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and extended his rule westwards to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. He struck coins in his name at Callatis and at first sought the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> against his enemies, but the two fell out. In 339 BCE he was killed, reportedly aged over ninety, in a battle with Philip's army near the Danube.</p></div>
